## Goal
Do the samples separate by experimental condition on the gene-level expression? A principal
component analysis of the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) from the gene counts,
plotted with points coloured by `condition`, as a sanity check on the design and on sample-level
outliers. This is exploratory; no statistical test is part of it.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The counts file is genes x samples with leading `gene_id`, `gene_name` columns, then A1..B3.
It is nf-core's length-scaled gene counts (tximport `lengthScaledTPM`), not raw integers. The
design supplies `condition` (untreated / treated) per `sample_id`.

## Method
All steps run in one script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` environment, using
pandas, numpy, scikit-learn and matplotlib. Inputs are read-only.

1. Resolve both inputs with `_system/resolve_artifact.py` (types `counts_gene`, `design`).
2. Read the counts; use `gene_id` as the index and keep only the sample columns. Read the
   design; require that its `sample_id` set equals the count columns exactly, else stop with
   an error (no silent dropping or reordering).
3. Filter genes: keep genes with a summed count >= 10 across the six samples.
4. Transform: `log2(count + 1)`.
5. Select the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples (all genes if fewer than 500
   pass the filter).
6. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on the samples x genes matrix: mean-centred per gene,
   not scaled to unit variance (the usual convention for expression PCA on log scale, as in
   DESeq2's `plotPCA`). Compute all components (at most 5 with 6 samples).
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition`, labelled with `sample_id`,
   with the percent variance explained on each axis. Colour-blind-safe two-colour palette,
   legend titled "condition". Save as PNG (200 dpi) and SVG.
8. Write the sample coordinates (all PCs, with `condition`) and the variance explained per PC.

Parameters fixed here (gene filter 10, top 500 genes, log2+1, no scaling) are the plan's
choices; changing any of them is a new analysis. No tool outside `gars-bio` is installed or
used. If scikit-learn or matplotlib is missing from `gars-bio`, execution is blocked on that
and is reported rather than worked around.

